- F-76 **The compiled checker accepts the specification's `comprises` example of the Meet Rule (`Specification/advanced/overloading.tex`, "The Meet Rule for Functions") by a coverage check used only in overload checking, not in its subtype test, and types a call whose argument's static type lies below closed traits and above the types that they list by the intersection of the return types of its most specific declarations, when they tie without coercion.** It builds P-60, which reads `comprises` at the level of values: several declarations below two overloads serve as their meet if together they cover it. Open: coverage refuses a pair when a declaration that it compares has static parameters (row 546). In an accepted family that has any other generic declaration, such a call is typed by the head of a sort, which follows declaration order and can vary between runs, not by the intersection (row 543). That narrower scope than P-60's is a default that waits for the curator's review (review-queue item 58). On the compiled path, a method call on a value typed by the intersection crashes the code generator (row 540), and a generic declaration instantiated at an intersection type dies as it loads (row 559). The checker checks a `comprises` clause only at the closed trait's immediate subtypes, so a type in another component below a generic subtrait escapes it (row 487). Its verdict on an overload set can depend on the components compiled before it in the same JVM (row 547). Gated by `compiler_tests/*Comprises*` and `*CoverageReturn*`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-return-type-rule/decision-record.md` sections 3 and 4.
  by: the clarity pass; note: rewritten for a reader without the batch (consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/facts/f6/proposals.md, "F-76"); the open items spelled out from their gap-ledger rows 546, 543, 540, 559, 487 and 547; "static type" and "stands between closed traits and their listed types" restored from P-60's wording; coverage said from P-60; after the check: the tie without coercion restored, as the text of 2026-10-13 had it (decision-record.md section 4; P-60); "its candidates" said as its most specific declarations; the coverage check kept out of the subtype test (P-60); rows 546 and 543 each given its condition and outcome, the sort said as declaration order, and row 543's scope said as a default (JUDGE.md D6; review-queue item 58) in place of "deliberate"; the specification's example located (row 492)
- F-79 **In the interpreter's library, `IntLiteral`, the type that the compiled checker gives an integer numeral, is declared on the model of the compiler's prelude: a sibling of the number types under `Number`, which each integer type, `QQ`, `RR64` and `AnyIntegral` convert from by coercion.** It builds the library half of P-76. Under walk a numeral is still a `ZZ32`, `ZZ64` or `ZZ`, so no numeral reaches these declarations until walk's half is built (F-74). `IntLiteral` declares itself the members of `Integral[\I\]` that a numeral could not reach, among them the getters `zero` and `one`, `odd`, `even`, `floor` and `DIVIDES`. There are three reasons: for some, no declaration of `Integral` applies to a sibling type even with coercion; for others, the declarations that apply by coercion tie; and a getter must be the type's own member. Each integer type declares its own comparisons, `CMP`, `MAXNUM` and `MINNUM`, and a `^` with an `IntLiteral` exponent. Without them, a typed integer with a numeral operand ties between a declaration inherited from a generic trait and the same operator's own declarations in `ZZ64`, `QQ` and `RR64`. The compiled checker refuses `(3).minimum` and `(3).maximum`, `ZZ32`'s getters (row 581). Gated under walk by `tests/IntegerOrderNumerals.fss`, which checks each integer type's own comparisons, `MAXNUM` and `MINNUM`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-numeral-library/REPORT.md` sections 4 and 6.
  by: the clarity pass; note: rewritten for a reader without the batch (consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/facts/f6/proposals.md, "F-79"); scoped to the compiled checker, with walk's numeral from P-76 and F-74; "the compiler library's IntLiteral" corrected to a type declared in the interpreter's library on the compiler prelude's model (P-76); the ties said from rung-numeral-library/REPORT.md sections 5 and 6; after the check: IntLiteral's own members said as more than zero and one (FortressBuiltin.fsi:180-221), with the report's three reasons in place of one (section 6 and its judge); the ties named against ZZ64's, QQ's and RR64's own; row 581's path said; the walk test's subject said from its header
- F-80 **The compiled checker judges two functional methods of one name, abstract declarations included, by the Meet Rule for Functional Methods in each type that provides both, unless their name is a symbol.** Such a type must provide their meet, compared without the `self` parameter. F-88 says what a type provides. An abstract functional method beside a concrete one in the same trait, with the same parameter types, is refused by the No Duplicates Rule. Unchecked: a pair that only an object expression provides (row 570), and every operator whose name is a symbol, such as `+`, `<`, `||` or a subscript, since the checker skips them (row 584; F-245 measures what the skip hides). It checks operators named by capitals, such as `CAP`, and eight named by words, `juxtaposition` among them. The checker's overloading memo is keyed on all the inputs of the check, so a pair accepted at the top level is not reused unchecked in a trait that provides it. Gated by `compiler_tests/FunctionalMethodMeet*`, and by the refusals that the specification asks for, `XXXFunctionalMethodMeetAbstractSibling` and `XXXNoDuplicatesRuleAbstract`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-overloading-checker/REPORT.md` sections 5 and 7; `compile-ladder/rung-checker-abstract-callsite/REPORT.md` section 1.
  by: the clarity pass; note: rewritten for a reader without the batch (consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/facts/f6/proposals.md, "F-80"); the operators that the checker skips added to the title and to what is unchecked (row 584; OverloadingChecker.scala:742-746 and NodeUtil.validOp; rung-checker-abstract-callsite/REPORT.md section 1); the memo's consequence said from rung-overloading-checker/REPORT.md section 7, which the detail now names; after the check: the skipped operators narrowed to those named by symbols, since validOp also admits eight words, juxtaposition among them (NodeUtil.java:1466-1482; row 585); the No Duplicates case placed in one trait; the two XXX tests said as refusals that the specification asks for
- F-81 **In the interpreter's library, every Meet Rule pair inside one type that the compiled checker checks has a declaration on its meet. The checker skips operators whose names are symbols (row 584), and some of their pairs have no meet.** `String`'s juxtaposition is three top-level operators, since two functional methods with `self` at different positions have no meet. `String`'s `||`, `|||`, `//` and `///` keep the shape that the specification refuses, `(a:Any, self)` beside `(self, b:Any)`, unseen while the checker skips them (row 585). F-245's measurement found other pairs without a meet, among them `Indexed`'s three subscripts in 17 types. The rung that added these meets also set the declared types listed in report section 1 to what their bodies answer. Elsewhere in the library some still differ, such as `__bigOperator2`'s fused branch (F-85). Gated by `tests/LibraryMeetDeclarations.fss`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-library-meets/REPORT.md` section 1.
